"Make it live" is five different designs, and picking the wrong one is how a feature that needed a 30-second poll acquires a reconnect bug that takes a quarter to stabilise.

## The five on the wire

![Polling, long polling, SSE, WebSocket and WebTransport compared](/diagrams/sysdesign/realtime-transports.svg)

## Choosing

The decision is almost entirely about **direction** and **frequency**:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A["Does the client need to<br/>send messages too?"] -->|Yes| WS["WebSocket"]
  A -->|No| B["How stale can it be?"]
  B -->|"Seconds are fine"| P["Polling"]
  B -->|"Must be immediate"| SSE["SSE"]
  WS --> C["Really? Typing indicators,<br/>cursors, acks?"]
  C -->|"No, just reading"| SSE
```

That last loop matters. Most "we need WebSockets" requirements are one-directional on inspection, and SSE gives you the same latency with automatic reconnection, plain HTTP semantics, and no custom protocol.

## Pros and cons, side by side

| | Direction | Wins on | Loses on |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Polling** | request/response | nothing to maintain — no connection means no reconnect code | a round trip per tick, most of them returning nothing |
| **Long polling** | server → client | works through anything, needs no protocol upgrade | full headers per message, a connection parked per client |
| **SSE** | server → client | reconnect, resume and plain HTTP for free | no upward channel, UTF-8 only |
| **WebSocket** | both | true full duplex, tiny frames, binary native | reconnect, resume, auth refresh and ordering are all yours |
| **WebTransport** | both | independent streams — one loss doesn't stall the rest | new, thin ecosystem, and UDP is often blocked |

## Polling — the default you should have to argue your way out of

```javascript
useQuery({
  queryKey: ["notifications"],
  queryFn: fetchNotifications,
  refetchInterval: 30_000,
  // Don't poll a tab nobody is looking at.
  refetchIntervalInBackground: false,
});
```

**Why you'd pick it.** There is no connection, so there is no connection to lose — no backoff, no resume cursor, no heartbeat, no proxy fallback. Every request is an ordinary `GET` you can read in the network tab and replay with `curl`, which means the whole feature is debuggable by anyone on the team rather than by whoever wrote the socket layer. Caching, auth and rate limiting are the ones you already have.

**What it costs.** Latency is bounded below by the interval — a 30-second poll means 15 seconds of average staleness, and tightening the interval is the only lever you have. Most ticks return `304` or an empty list, so the majority of the traffic is pure waste, and that waste multiplies by client count: 10,000 clients on a 5-second interval is 2,000 requests per second to say "nothing happened". On mobile each tick also wakes the radio, which is a battery cost the user feels and you don't see.

## Long polling — the compatibility fallback, not the choice

The client sends a request, the server *doesn't answer* until it has something to say, the client immediately re-requests. It simulates a push over ordinary request/response.

**Why you'd pick it.** Universal reach. There is no `Upgrade` handshake and no unusual content type, so it survives every proxy, corporate TLS-inspecting middlebox, ancient load balancer and server framework in existence. That makes it the natural degraded mode when a WebSocket or SSE negotiation fails — and libraries like Socket.IO ship it for exactly that reason.

**What it costs.** Every message carries a complete HTTP request and response — headers, cookies, often several hundred bytes to deliver a 40-byte payload. Between a reply and the next request there is a gap in which the client is not listening, so messages arriving in that window are either delayed or dropped depending on how the server buffers. And the server holds an open, idle connection per client anyway, so you pay the memory of a persistent transport without getting one; that's before the connection churn of tearing down and re-establishing on every message.

## SSE — the underrated one

```javascript
const es = new EventSource("/api/stream");
es.addEventListener("post", (e) => applyPost(JSON.parse(e.data)));
// The browser reconnects on its own and replays Last-Event-ID.
es.onerror = () => setConnectionState("reconnecting");
```

**Why you'd pick it.** SSE is a normal HTTP response that never ends (`Content-Type: text/event-stream`), so everything already in the path keeps working: your auth cookies, your [CDN](/system-design/115), your WAF, your load balancer, your [observability](/system-design/111). Nothing needs a special configuration for it.

The resilience is the real argument. `EventSource` is a browser primitive, so reconnection, backoff and liveness are implemented *below* your code — when the connection drops the browser reopens it on its own and replays the `Last-Event-ID` header, so if the server writes an `id:` on every event it can resume from precisely where the client stopped. That is the single hardest part of a WebSocket, and here it is free and correct by default. The wire format is human-readable text, so the whole stream is legible in the network tab.

**What it costs.** It is strictly one-way. Client→server means an ordinary `POST` on a separate request, which is fine for chat messages but awkward if the upward traffic is high-frequency (cursor positions, typing indicators) because each one is now a request with full headers. It is UTF-8 only, so binary has to be Base64'd — about a 33% size penalty plus an encode/decode on both ends. And on HTTP/1.1 the open stream permanently occupies one of the browser's ~6 connections per origin, so a few tabs of your app can starve the rest of the page; on [HTTP/2](/browser/161) or HTTP/3 the streams are multiplexed and the limit disappears, which is why "use SSE" and "be on HTTP/2" are effectively the same decision.

## WebSocket — when the client genuinely talks back

```javascript
// The half that is actually the work:
function connect(attempt = 0) {
  const ws = new WebSocket(url);
  ws.onopen = () => {
    attempt = 0;
    resubscribe();
    flushOutbox();
  };
  ws.onclose = () => {
    // Exponential backoff with jitter — without jitter every client
    // reconnects at the same instant and takes the server down again.
    const wait = Math.min(30_000, 2 ** attempt * 1000) * (0.5 + Math.random());
    setTimeout(() => connect(attempt + 1), wait);
  };
  ws.onmessage = (e) => apply(JSON.parse(e.data));
}
```

**Why you'd pick it.** One TCP connection carries traffic in both directions simultaneously, with no request/response pairing — the client can send a cursor position while the server is mid-way through pushing a document patch. After the opening handshake the per-message overhead is a 2–14 byte frame header rather than a full set of HTTP headers, so at high message rates it is an order of magnitude cheaper on the wire than anything request-based. It carries `ArrayBuffer` and `Blob` natively, so binary protocols, [CRDT](/system-design/204) updates and audio chunks go over it without Base64. And because the server can address a specific open socket, "push to this one user right now" is a direct operation instead of a lookup-and-wait.

**What it costs.** The `new WebSocket(url)` line is the easy part; the design around it is the actual work:

| Concern | What you must decide |
| --- | --- |
| **Reconnect** | [Exponential backoff](/system-design/410) **with jitter** — a thundering herd will re-kill a recovering server |
| **Liveness** | TCP won't tell you the connection died silently; you need ping/pong and a timeout |
| **Resume** | Resume from the last message id, never from "now" — otherwise every reconnect silently drops messages |
| **Auth** | Tokens expire mid-connection; decide whether you re-auth in-band or reconnect |
| **Ordering** | Messages can arrive out of order after a reconnect; sequence them |
| **Backpressure** | A fast server and a slow client means an unbounded queue; drop or coalesce |
| **Fan-out** | Many tabs → a `SharedWorker` or leader election, not one socket per tab |
| **Fallback** | Corporate proxies still break WebSockets; degrade to polling |

There is an infrastructure cost too. `ws://` starts as an HTTP `Upgrade`, and plenty of L7 load balancers, API gateways and enterprise proxies either refuse it or terminate the connection on an idle timeout they apply to ordinary HTTP — which is why the heartbeat exists. Because the connection is stateful, your servers are now stateful: sticky routing or a shared pub/sub backplane (Redis, NATS) so that any node can reach a socket held by another. And nothing about the protocol gives you the semantics — no retry, no ack, no method, no status code — so you end up designing a small message protocol of your own and versioning it.

> "The socket is a day. Reconnect, resume, auth refresh, ordering and the proxy fallback are the quarter. That's why I'd only pay it if the client genuinely sends messages."

## WebTransport — the one to watch

Built on HTTP/3 and QUIC, which run over UDP.

**Why you'd pick it.** WebSocket is one ordered byte stream over TCP, which means head-of-line blocking: a single lost packet stalls *everything* behind it until it is retransmitted, even messages that had nothing to do with it. QUIC gives you many independent streams inside one connection, so a loss stalls only its own stream. It also offers unreliable *datagrams* alongside reliable streams — for cursor positions or a game's position updates, a dropped packet should be skipped, not retransmitted late, and this is the first browser transport that lets you say so. Connections survive a network change (Wi-Fi to cellular) because QUIC identifies a connection by an ID rather than by the IP/port pair, and the handshake is 0-RTT on a resumption.

**What it costs.** Reach and maturity. Safari support arrived late, server support means running an HTTP/3 stack, and the library ecosystem is thin next to the decade of WebSocket tooling. More practically, plenty of corporate networks and public Wi-Fi block or [throttle](/coding/14) UDP on 443, so a production deployment needs a WebSocket fallback anyway — which means building both.

## The reconnect, step by step

The thing every transport choice eventually reduces to. Watch what `Last-Event-ID` (or your own cursor) is actually buying:

```trace
{
  "input": "an event feed drops for 4 seconds mid-stream",
  "lanes": [
    { "id": "conn", "label": "connection", "kind": "phases", "of": ["open", "streaming", "dropped", "backoff", "resumed"] },
    { "id": "ui", "label": "what the UI shows", "kind": "log" }
  ],
  "vars": ["lastId", "backoff", "outbox"],
  "steps": [
    {
      "note": "Stream opens. The server writes an `id:` on every event, so the client always knows where it is.",
      "cursors": { "conn": { "at": "open" } },
      "marks": { "conn": { "open": "active" } },
      "cells": { "ui": ["live"] },
      "vars": { "lastId": "—", "backoff": "0ms", "outbox": "[]" }
    },
    {
      "note": "Events 41 and 42 arrive and are applied. `lastId` advances with them.",
      "cursors": { "conn": { "at": "streaming" } },
      "marks": { "conn": { "open": "done", "streaming": "active" } },
      "cells": { "ui": ["live", "applied #41", "applied #42"] },
      "vars": { "lastId": "42", "backoff": "0ms", "outbox": "[]" }
    },
    {
      "note": "The connection drops. Events 43–45 are published while the client is gone — this is where resuming from \"now\" loses them silently.",
      "cursors": { "conn": { "at": "dropped" } },
      "marks": { "conn": { "streaming": "done", "dropped": "bad" } },
      "cells": { "ui": ["live", "applied #41", "applied #42", "⚠ reconnecting…"] },
      "vars": { "lastId": "42", "backoff": "0ms", "outbox": "[draft-msg]" }
    },
    {
      "note": "Backoff with **jitter** — `1s × (0.5 + random())`. Without the jitter every client in the fleet retries on the same tick and re-kills the server that is trying to come back.",
      "cursors": { "conn": { "at": "backoff" } },
      "marks": { "conn": { "dropped": "done", "backoff": "window" } },
      "cells": { "ui": ["live", "applied #41", "applied #42", "⚠ reconnecting…"] },
      "vars": { "lastId": "42", "backoff": "1.4s", "outbox": "[draft-msg]" }
    },
    {
      "note": "Reconnect sends `Last-Event-ID: 42`. The server replays 43, 44, 45 — nothing was lost, and the queued write flushes with its idempotency key so a duplicate retry is harmless.",
      "cursors": { "conn": { "at": "resumed" } },
      "marks": { "conn": { "backoff": "done", "resumed": "hit" } },
      "cells": { "ui": ["live", "applied #41", "applied #42", "replayed #43 #44 #45", "sent draft-msg"] },
      "vars": { "lastId": "45", "backoff": "0ms", "outbox": "[]" }
    }
  ],
  "result": "no gap in the feed — the cursor, not the clock, decides where the stream restarts"
}
```

With SSE the browser does steps 4 and 5 for you. With WebSocket, every one of them is code you write and test.

## Batching — the part everyone forgets

At high frequency the transport stops being the bottleneck and rendering does. 200 messages per second against a 60 fps budget means you are three times over before React runs.

```javascript
// Coalesce into one render per frame.
let pending = new Map();
let scheduled = false;

function onMessage(msg) {
  pending.set(msg.id, msg); // last write wins per entity
  if (scheduled) return;
  scheduled = true;
  requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    applyBatch(pending);
    pending = new Map();
    scheduled = false;
  });
}
```

Two wins at once: at most one render per frame, and superseded updates for the same entity are collapsed instead of each causing a render.

## Always design the disconnected state

Whatever you choose, the UI must answer "am I live right now?"

- A visible connection indicator once reconnection has been failing for a few seconds
- Reads fall back to the last known cache rather than emptying the screen
- Writes queue locally and flush on reconnect, with idempotency keys so retries do not duplicate
- On resume, reconcile from a cursor — never assume you have everything

## Common mistakes

- **WebSocket by default.** You have bought a reconnect problem for a one-way feed.
- **Reconnect without jitter.** Every client reconnects simultaneously and re-kills the server.
- **Resuming from "now".** Messages during the outage are silently lost; the UI is wrong with no error.
- **SSE on HTTP/1.1 without counting connections.** Three tabs and the rest of the page stops loading.
- **No heartbeat on a WebSocket.** A proxy closed it thirty seconds ago and nobody has noticed.
- **No batching.** The transport is fine and the main thread is on fire.
- **Polling a hidden tab.** Wasted battery and wasted origin capacity.

## Key takeaways

- Direction decides it: one-way is SSE, two-way is WebSocket, tolerant of staleness is polling.
- SSE gives reconnection, resume and plain HTTP for free; WebSocket makes all three your problem — and pays you back in full duplex, tiny frames and native binary.
- Long polling is a fallback you keep for reach, not a design you choose.
- WebTransport removes head-of-line blocking and adds unreliable datagrams, but UDP blocking means you ship a WebSocket fallback beside it.
- Backoff must have jitter; resume must use a cursor.
- Coalesce updates into `requestAnimationFrame` — at high frequency, rendering is the bottleneck.
- Design the disconnected state explicitly; it is part of the feature.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"What happens when the connection drops for two minutes?"** — Backoff with jitter, resume from the last event id, reconcile against the cache, and show the reader they were disconnected.
- **"1,000 messages a second?"** — The transport is fine. Coalesce per entity into one render per frame, and consider moving parsing to a worker.
- **"Why not WebSocket, it's more powerful?"** — Power you do not use is cost you still pay: reconnect, resume, auth refresh, ordering, proxy fallback.
- **"When is SSE the wrong answer?"** — High-rate client→server traffic, binary payloads, or an HTTP/1.1-only path where the connection cap bites.
- **"What does WebTransport give you that WebSocket doesn't?"** — Independent streams, so one lost packet doesn't stall the others, plus unreliable datagrams for data that is worthless late.
